# The CONCEPT DOI, which always resolves to the newest release. The
# per-version DOI (10.5281/zenodo.22211591 for v0.3.1) is minted by Zenodo
# at each GitHub release and is recorded in README's Citing section; it
# must not sit here, where it would name an older release than `version`.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22068632
repository-code: "https://github.com/mojolearn/mojolearn"
abstract: >-
  A GPU machine-learning library written in Mojo, and the first one to give
  the same source three vendors and the same BITS across them. Its
  distinguishing result is a three-tier numeric contract selected at runtime,
  where the strictest tier produces bit-identical answers on Apple Silicon
  via Metal, NVIDIA via CUDA and AMD via ROCm, verified by per-stage identity
  cards diffed across the three at pinned commits. No comparable GPU library
  offers such a tier, because the CUDA implementations in the field were
  never asked to run anywhere but CUDA.
  Every line of Mojo in this repository was written for it. That includes the
  determinism ladder and its pinned-arithmetic primitives, the Metal backend,
  the host-side control plane every estimator is reached through, the identity
  cards and host oracles and the sabotage methodology that gates them, the
  bit-identical FP32 GEMM contract, portable elementary functions that replace
  the host math library, a portable model serialization format, and the
  transformer and Mamba identity work.
  The estimator surface covers gradient-boosted oblivious trees, random
  forests, extremely randomized trees, k-means, brute-force and IVF nearest
  neighbors, DBSCAN, HDBSCAN, PCA, UMAP, ridge and logistic regression,
  coordinate descent, support vector classification, kernel ridge regression,
  Gaussian processes, Gaussian mixtures, kernel density estimation,
  single-linkage and spectral clustering, isolation forests, Holt-Winters,
  ARIMA, scoring metrics, and transformer and Mamba training and inference.
keywords:
  - machine learning
  - GPU
  - Metal
  - Apple Silicon
  - Mojo
  - clustering
  - k-means
  - DBSCAN
  - nearest neighbors
  - PCA
  - gradient boosting
  - oblivious trees
